    if (fd_i2c < 0) {
        perror("Failed to open I2C device");
        fclose(log_file);
        teardown_gpio();
        return 1;
    }

    while (1) {
        ret = i2c_smbus_read_word_data(fd_i2c, 0x1e << 1);
        if (ret < 0) {
            perror("Failed to read temperature");
            continue;
        }

        temp = (double)ret / 100.0;
        if (temp < TEMP_THRESHOLD_LOW || temp > TEMP_THRESHOLD_HIGH) {
            perror("Temperature out of range");
            continue;
        }

        ret = gpiod_line_set_value(line, !!(uint32_t)map_temperature(temp));
        if (ret < 0) {
            perror("Failed to set PWM duty cycle");
            continue;
        }

        clock_gettime(CLOCK_REALTIME, &ts);
        if (fprintf(log_file, "%lld,%.1f,%.0f\n", ts.tv_sec, temp, map_temperature(temp)) < 0) {
            perror("Failed to write log file row");
            continue;
        }

        sleep(5);
    }

    close(fd_i2c);
    fclose(log_file);
    teardown_gpio();

    return 0;
}
```

This code initializes the GPIO line, opens the I2C device for temperature reading, and reads the temperature every 5 seconds. It maps the temperature to a PWM duty cycle and writes the temperature and duty cycle to a log file. The program handles errors and cleans up resources on SIGINT or SIGTERM by turning the fan off.

Please note that this code assumes you have a temperature sensor connected to the I2C bus on the Raspberry Pi, and it uses the AK8965 sensor as an example. You may need to adjust the I2C address and the sensor-specific read function according to your sensor.

Also, don't forget to install the libgpiod library on your Raspberry Pi before compiling the code:

```sh
sudo apt-get install libgpiod-dev
```

Compile the code with:

```sh
gcc -o fan_controller fan_controller.c -lgpiod -lrt
```

Run the compiled binary with:

```sh
./fan_controller
```

