        if (head != tail) {
            i2c_read(&i2c, ACC_XOUT_H, (uint8_t *)&buffer[head].x, 6);
            gyro_data_to_rad(&buffer[head], rad);
            head = (head + 1) % BUFFER_SIZE;
        }

        gpiod_line_get_value(gpiod_get_byname("gpiochip0", 17), &line_data.value);
        if (line_data.value == i2c.line_state) {
            continue;
        }
        gpiod_line_set_value(gpiod_get_byname("gpiochip0", 17), i2c.line_state);
        break;
    }

    gpiod_line_release_request(&line_data);
    gpiod_chip_close(GPIOD_CHIP_CLOSE_FLAG_NONE);
    close(i2c.fd);

    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```

This code initializes the I2C bus, selects the MPU-6050, reads data from the gyroscope, stores it in a circular buffer, and invokes a user-defined callback when the absolute angular velocity exceeds 50.0 degrees/second. It also includes robust error handling for I2C communication failures and gracefully handles a SIGINT (Ctrl+C) signal.

To compile the code, you can use the following command:

```sh
gcc -o mpu6050_gyro mpu6050_gyro.c -lrt -lgpiod -lwiringPi -lwiringPiDev
```

Please note that you need to have the following libraries installed on your Raspberry Pi:

- libgpiod-dev
- libwiringPi-dev
- libwiringPi

You can install them using the following command:

```sh
sudo apt-get install libgpiod-dev libwiringPi-dev libwiringPi
```

However, I'd like to clarify that the code uses the wiringPi library for GPIO access, which is an older library. If you prefer to use the more modern libgpiod library, you can modify the code accordingly. The changes would be minimal, as the code for accessing the GPIO lines remains the same. The only difference would be the initialization and cleanup of the GPIO lines.

For libgpiod, you can use the following command to compile the code:

```sh
