    for (int i = 0; i < NUM_RELAYS; ++i) {
        gpiod_line_release(lines[i]);
    }

    gpiod_chip_close(gpiod_chip);
}

void handle_signal(int sig) {
    cleanup();
    exit(0);
}

int main(void) {
    signal(SIGINT, handle_signal);
    signal(SIGTERM, handle_signal);

    if (init_gpio() < 0) {
        return 1;
    }

    if (init_pwm() < 0) {
        cleanup();
        return 1;
    }

    for (int duty_cycle = 0; duty_cycle <= TOTAL_DUTY_CYCLE; duty_cycle += DUTY_CYCLE_STEP) {
        for (int i = 0; i < NUM_RELAYS; ++i) {
            gpiod_line_set_value(lines[i], duty_cycle <= TOTAL_DUTY_CYCLE - DUTY_CYCLE_STEP);
        }

        sleep(1);
        log_event("Duty cycle updated");
    }

    cleanup();
    return 0;
}
```

This code initializes the GPIO pins and PWM, sets up signal handlers for SIGINT and SIGTERM, and then gradually increases the duty cycle of the PWM signals for each relay over a 30-minute period. It logs every event with a timestamp to the `smart_lighting.log` file.

Please note that you need to have the `libgpiod` library installed on your Raspberry Pi to compile and run this code. You can install it using the following command:

```sh
sudo apt-get install libgpiod-dev
```

Compile the code with:

```sh
gcc -o smart_lighting smart_lighting.c -lgpiod -lpthread
```

And run it with:

```sh
./smart_lighting
```

You can stop the program by pressing Ctrl+C.

